{"type":"text","timestamp":1782354994817,"sessionID":"ses_10364f608ffe3SNGhX4sWtXwgr","part":{"id":"prt_efca25f8a001J9B7xGOxhP1zwG","messageID":"msg_efca257100013lQCDjvCMkBdKR","sessionID":"ses_10364f608ffe3SNGhX4sWtXwgr","type":"text","text":"    I should verify the server starts without errors quickly. Let me run a quick smoke with stdin immediate close? Since stdio server will wait for input, I can spawn and close input, or test that the file runs and loads data within some timeout. Maybe easier: run `node dist/index.js --help`? My script doesn't handle help, it'll hang. Could create a small smoke test that imports `main`? main blocks connecting stdio, not ideal. We can test server building with tools schema perhaps via importing server file. It will execute top-level main() though; that will do loadData then connect stdio - in test maybe not good. But build was OK. \n\nMaybe update README to provide run instructions. Not required. Let me just ensure all main files exist and maybe do `npx tsc --noEmit`? Build already does emit. Good.\n\nOne improvement: add .gitignore for node_modules/dist. Not strictly required. But it is good practice. Not commanded. I'll skip.\n\nActually I should double-check project files: package.json, tsconfig.json, src/types.ts, src/normalize.ts, src/loader.ts, src/engine.ts, src/format.ts, src/index.ts, vitest.config.ts, test/engine.test.ts. That covers it.\n\nDouble-check import of canonicalTeamKey in loader.ts: exported from normalize and re-exported at bottom of loader.ts. Not used inside loader. It's okay.\n\nPotential issue: the Object.values(standings) could include teams with 0 matches? Only added if they appear, so ok.\n\nPotential issue: in filterMatches, sorting by date desc but if both dates undefined no stable order. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: in search_players tool, `club` normalization uses normalizeTeamName; for FIFA clubs, many are European like Paris Saint-Germain unaffected. For Brazilian clubs, handles variations. Good.\n\nPotential issue: The `team` filter in engine uses normalizeTeamName to compute canonicalTeamKey, and canonicalTeamKey removes accents? Lowercase preserves Unicode. Searching for \"São Paulo\" -> \"são paulo\". Data normalized to \"São Paulo\". Lowercase matches. Good.\n\nBut canonicalTeamKey also strips state suffix for names such as \"Flamengo-RJ\". Good. \n\nPotential issue: The Copa do Brasil data had team names like \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\". Our normalization removes parenthetical then strips state suffix? Wait string: \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\". Regex first removes state suffix at end: pattern `\\\\s*[-–]\\\\s*(AC|...|RJ)\\\\s*$` -> matches \" - RJ\" at end, removes, leaving \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\". Then removes parenthetical -> \"Boavista Sport Club\". Fine.\n\nPotential issue: In some rows state suffix is part of name after parenthetical? Already handled.\n\nPotential issue: competitions from BR-Football-Dataset include \"Copa do Brasil\" and \"Brasileirão\" etc. normalizeCompetition detects. Good.\n\nPotential issue: TheStats DB not integrated; optional. Not required.\n\nPotential issue: Performance: loadData reads all 6 CSVs (approx 25 MB) synchronously at startup. All tests load at beforeAll. The spec says simple lookups <2 sec; async load not measured. Query operations are in-memory loops over 24k matches; should be fast. Good.\n\nPotential issue: MCP SDK version 1.0 may have different import paths. I used \"^1.0.0\" which installed latest. Build succeeded. Let me check actual installed match. from package-lock? Node_modules imports resolved during build. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Server index.ts top-level main() calls process.exit on error. When imported, it will execute main, which blocks. In tests engine.ts directly imported. If someone imports index.ts, side effects. Only the server entry point. Fine.\n\nMaybe add a `.prettierrc`? Not required.\n\nConsider adding TSDoc? Not necessary.\n\nI should summarize final state to user. Use same language as user (English).\n\nBefore final, maybe commit? User didn't ask git. Don't.\n\nLet me check number of tests pass without data dir env? Tests passed. Good.\n\nFinal answer: concise summary. </think> Done. I implemented the Brazilian Soccer MCP server in TypeScript.\n\nFiles created:\n\n- `package.json` / `tsconfig.json` / `vitest.config.ts`\n- `src/types.ts` — data models\n- `src/normalize.ts` — team name, competition, date, and number normalization\n- `src/loader.ts` — loads all 6 CSVs into a unified `Match[]` and `Player[]`\n- `src/engine.ts` — query engine for matches, team stats, head-to-head, standings, players, and aggregate stats\n- `src/format.ts` — human-readable output formatting\n- `src/index.ts` — MCP server exposing `search_matches`, `team_stats`, `head_to_head`, `competition_standings`, `search_players`, and `competition_stats`\n- `test/engine.test.ts` — BDD-style tests covering data loading, match/team/player/competition/stat queries\n\nVerification:\n\n- `npm run build` — compiles successfully\n- `npm test` — **10/10 tests pass**\n\nThe server is started with:\n\n```bash\nnpm run build\nnpm start\n# or directly:\nnode dist/index.js\n```\n\nIt loads the Kaggle CSVs automatically from `data/kaggle/` and speaks MCP over stdio.","time":{"start":1782354960266,"end":1782354994802}}}
